.docname {Alerting}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Alert | The message sent for one Breach | "Lab 2 is too hot" |
    | Alert Type | A kind of alert | Temperature |
    | Alert Choice | What one person wants, in one organization | Sam, Northfield: temperature yes, air no |
    | Device | One place a person can be reached | Sam's phone |
    | Recipient | A person who gets this alert | Sam |
    | Breach | The time a room spends outside a limit | Lab 2, from 14:05 to 14:20 |
    | Opt in | Choosing to receive something | Sam opted in to temperature alerts |

**Question:** who must be told, and where? **Kind:** Supporting. **Needs:** [Identity & Access](domain-identity.qd), [Building Management](domain-buildings.qd), [Telemetry Ingestion](domain-ingestion.qd).

This domain never decides that something is wrong. Ingestion did that already. It only gets the news to the right people.

---

## In the real world

Lab 2 is too hot. Sam should hear about it, on the phone, even when not following the building.

Two different questions hide here:

- **What** does Sam want to hear about?
- **Where** can Sam be reached?

They change separately. A new phone must not reset Sam's choices (AL-6).

---

## The model

.mermaid
    classDiagram
        class Device {
            person
        }
        class AlertChoice {
            person
            organization
            types
        }
        class Alert {
            breach
            type
        }
        Alert "1" ..> "many" Device : sent to

Device and Alert Choice are two separate things on purpose. Each one can be replaced without touching the other.

---

## Who gets it

Each step narrows the list. Numbers are made up.

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        A["Breach Started in Lab 2"] --> B["Science Hall belongs to northfield-university"]
        B --> C["All 40 members of the organization"]
        C --> D["12 chose temperature alerts"]
        D --> E["9 have at least one device"]
        E --> F["14 devices get the alert"]

The first step is the one question this domain asks: "which organizations own this building?". Ownership is a Building Management fact (AL-7).

---

## Worked example

| Person | Member? | Chose temperature? | Devices | Gets it? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Sam | Northfield | Yes | Phone, tablet | Yes, on both |
| Ada | Northfield | No | Phone | No |
| Lia | Northfield | Yes | None | No, nowhere to send it |
| Max | Acme | Yes | Phone | No, wrong organization |

Lia can see the problem by following the building. Alerts add to that, they do not replace it.

---

## Rules

1. One Breach, one Alert. Readings during the same breach send nothing new (AL-3).
2. Only members of the building's organizations, and only those who opted in (AL-7).
3. A person has one Alert Choice per organization. Choices do not leak across (AL-5).
4. An Alert goes to all of a person's devices (AL-3).
5. Removing a device keeps the choices and the other devices (AL-6).

**Edge case: shared building.** Science Hall belongs to two organizations, and Sam is in both. Sam gets one alert, not two. It is one breach.

**Edge case: back to normal.** The room recovers before anyone reacts. Nothing extra is sent. No "ignore that" message (AL-3).

---

## Open questions

- Does anyone need to acknowledge an alert?
- If nobody opted in, is it fine that nobody hears?
- A new member: are alerts on or off at the start? AL-7 says "opted in", so off.
